# =============================================================================
# inf — host-only in kernels
# =============================================================================
#
# What this teaches:
#   • A kernel parameter, return, local or field must have a device layout.
#     `inf` does not, so it is reported as a host-only type
#     (`nucleum_host_type`) by `faber check`, before any device lowering.
#   • Narrow on the host and pass the fixed-width result: `x ↦ u64 ⊥ 0`.
#
# Common mistakes:
#   • passing an exact big value straight to a kernel — kernels work on
#     fixed-width numbers.
#
# See also: inf, nucleum, publica
# =============================================================================

# kernel/inf-param-error — an `inf` kernel parameter
#
# WHY: pins F9 rulings 39(b) and 47 beside the other kernel legality rules.
#
# EXPECTED OUTPUT:
#   inf-param-error.expected — the stable diagnostic identity.

@ kernel
@ public
fn launch(inf lane) → void {
}

main {
    print 1
}
